From 09b74d49ddb71f8ba16b81589e4c87cd6a5ef7c4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 23:09:22 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 172/209] fix: the digital film grain sparkled in the dark -
 sensor noise is additive, not a perturbation in stops

A dark CMOS frame at ISO 777 came back peppered with sparse, saturated single-pixel
magenta / cyan / white dots. CCD showed the same thing as column streaks, Phone as
heavier sparkles. Film was correct.

Diagnosis. FilmGrainSensorField returned the noise as a perturbation in STOPS,
sigmaStops = sigmaRaw * attenuation / (x * ln2), and the caller applied it through the
film family's multiplicative blend x' = x * exp2(g*n - bias). Sensor noise in the
shadows is ADDITIVE - read noise, DSNU and the row / column terms are fixed electron
counts that do not know the pixel's value, and shot noise is sqrt(e) - so dividing by
a value that goes to zero makes the relative term diverge. The FILMGRAIN_MAX_SIGMA_STOPS
1.5 cap bounded sigma but not the product: the unit-variance field n = delta / sigmaRaw
is itself unbounded in the deep shadows, because the Poisson branch (e < 20 e-) draws
integer electron counts against a sub-electron sigma, so n of 5 to 10 is ordinary there.
Ten stops and more of g*n reached exp2, and a near-black pixel came out bright and
coloured by the per-channel raw white-balance gains. A real sensor's black is a lifted,
slightly grey floor with a faint chroma blotch, never sparkles.

The model change. The digital families now have their own path:

  FilmGrainSensorField returns delta in DISPLAY-LINEAR units (the model already computed
  delta = k * dE * wb; the photosite / stacking attenuation is applied to it, and the
  chroma NR luma/chroma split is done in the same linear units) plus sigmaLin, the
  analytic sigma of delta after attenuation, for the report and for the "anything to
  apply" test.

  The blend for digital is x' = clamp(x + amount * delta, 0, 1). No log, no exp2, no
  Jensen bias term (additive zero-mean noise is mean-preserving in linear by
  construction; the encoded mean shifts by a second-order concave term far under one
  code value, ignored on purpose and documented), and FILMGRAIN_MAX_SIGMA_STOPS is gone.

  The clamp at 0 lifts the deepest black. That is what a real sensor's black-level
  pedestal exists to absorb; no pedestal is modelled, so the lifted black stays and is
  documented rather than cancelled.

  Above the clip point there is still no noise. New: a pixel that is identically 0 in
  all three channels is also left alone on the digital path - that is the anamorphic
  gate mask's letterbox bar (PostAA.cfx:1016), and additive read noise, unlike a
  multiplicative one, would otherwise put grain on it.

The film path is untouched: same cell grid, same R(t), same rho mixing, same
x * exp2(g*n - g*g*0.06017) blend, same bit-identical untouched-pixel path.

Debug. r_FilmGrainDebug 1 (grey card) and 2 (response strip) keep working; the strip
now plots sigmaLin / (x * ln2) for digital, the same quantity the old code used to
APPLY and which is now only ever displayed, so the digital curve rises toward the dark
end and pins to the top of the strip where the read floor is louder than the signal.
The debug-3 report for digital was rewritten around the additive model: sigma in code
values at the card value AND four stops under it, in a shadow, because a constant number
of electrons is a constant number of linear units and the sRGB encoding turns that into
many code values in the shadows and few in the highlights. One number at mid grey said
nothing about where the noise lives.

New knob. FILMGRAIN_INTEGRATION_EXP (the sensor -> output integration exponent) is no
longer only a #define: it travels on the bus as Grain_User_Digital3.x, registered,
saved and restored like the other grain vectors, and carried per family in the preset
table. Default unchanged at 0.7; 1.0 is the textbook variance / N and 0.0 turns the
integration off - one photosite's noise on one output pixel whatever the downscale,
the "pixel peeping at 1:1" look, worth about 2.6x the default on a 6K sensor shown at
1528 px. A negative value selects the shader's compile-time fallback.

No %_RT_ flag, no RunTime.ext change, no new pass, no new texture, no change to the
film generator.

Docs in the same commit: FilmGrainSpec.md section 3 (the two blends, why they differ,
the mean and the clamp) and section 3.4 (additive output units, the sigma formula, the
integration exponent as a preset value) plus the section 4 bus table; README.md's
digital sensor model subsection; docs/ConsoleReference.md for r_FilmGrainDebug 2 / 3
and the Grain_User_Digital3 layout.

